{
  "brief_rationale": "The doctor immediately recognized the postpartum headache with prior hypertension/eclampsia-risk as a high‑risk emergency (RC1), performed comprehensive danger‑sign screening covering blood pressure, severe headache, visual disturbance, seizure, consciousness changes, and chest pain (RC2), gave a clear “now” emergency threshold (RC3), did not downgrade risk despite uncertain home BP (RC4), prohibited self‑adjustment of medications (RC5), addressed caregiver conflict by proposing a clear division of labor (RC6), instructed what key information to bring and tell the receiving team (RC7), and obtained a teach‑back of the next steps including who does what and when (RC8). All responsibility‑chain items are met without any serious or minor error. Two high‑order points—HO2 (executable family plan) and HO4 (bounded closure with teach‑back)—were triggered and actively completed with meaningful trajectory impact, while the remaining HO1 and HO3 were also triggered and partially or fully integrated. No important non‑chain defect is present. The trajectory closes safely with a valid, executable, and traceable plan. Therefore the grade is Strong (4).",
